<ResourceDictionary xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
                    xmlns:sys="clr-namespace:System;assembly=System.Runtime">

    <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         SCHEME — the keys the app's own surfaces bind to for DATA.

         Everything on an app surface that paints data resolves one of these,
         always through {DynamicResource}. PresetService overwrites them in
         Application.Resources from the dashboard's preset. Desktop widgets do
         not use them: each one resolves its own preset, because each may wear
         a different one.

         They live HERE, in the dictionary that is always merged, rather than
         in a profile — profiles are files on disk that users add and delete,
         and a key that binds to nothing is a control that renders blank.
         This file is the guarantee that every role always exists.

         The values below are only the pre-load fallback, so the first frame
         has something to draw before the preset is published. They are Ember's
         colours written out by hand, because XAML cannot read the JSON — the
         one duplication here that cannot be removed, so change both or neither.

         The hardware-named tokens that used to live here — Aquila.Cpu,
         Aquila.Chart.Gpu and the rest — are gone. Their colours were not
         lost: they are Presets/classic.json, selectable like any other.
         ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

    <!-- Window scrim, over the backdrop. Transparent here on purpose: this is what the FLUENT style
         gets, and Fluent means plain Windows material with nothing of ours on top — the same rule that
         already gives it no overlay and no accent of ours. The Aquila themes replace it with a tint.

         It must exist here regardless: a theme overlay can be swapped out, and a key that vanishes from
         under a DynamicResource would leave the window with no background at all. -->
    <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         SPACING — a named scale, never free margins.

         A theme that can put any margin anywhere stops painting badly and starts breaking layouts. Four
         steps and a corner radius are enough to make a theme feel denser or airier without letting it
         move anything.
         ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

    <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         CHROME — what the APPLICATION says about itself.

         Separate from Aquila.Scheme.* on purpose, and the separation is the point. Scheme is what a
         READING looks like: normal, elevated, alert, critical, decided per tick from the value. Chrome is
         what the app looks like when it is telling you something about itself — a heading, a warning
         triangle beside a widget that cannot be drawn, a Delete that cannot be undone.

         They were the same colours until now, because chrome had nowhere else to go: roughly half the
         uses of Aquila.Scheme.Accent and nearly every use of Aquila.Scheme.Alert are chrome borrowing the
         data palette. None of those mean "this reading is hot"; they mean "this is a problem". #44 moves
         them here.

         Keeping them apart is what lets a preset paint critical purple without the app's own Delete
         button going purple with it.
         ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

    <!-- No control face, outline or text colour here, and that is the point.

         They were here, as Aquila.Control.Fill / .Stroke / .Text, and it was a mistake twice over. They
         were a second name for something the kit already names — ControlFillColorDefaultBrush and its two
         companions, which the Aquila theme was ALREADY overriding — so there were two sources of truth for
         one outline, and they disagreed through three rounds of fixes. And a colour stated HERE reaches
         Fluent, where the rule is that Fluent gets nothing of ours: a dark field on a light Fluent window,
         which is exactly what shipped.

         The rules in Controls.xaml resolve the kit's keys instead. Under Fluent that is the kit's own
         value, light or dark, as it should be; under Aquila the theme overrides it. -->

    <!-- A control that is CHOSEN. The FALLBACK only: an Aquila theme replaces both from its own palette,
         beside the accent they are shades of. What is left here is what Fluent gets, and what any theme
         gets that forgets to say — a key that vanished from under a DynamicResource would leave the chosen
         state with no fill at all. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="Aquila.Control.ChosenFill" Color="#1FFF6B35"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="Aquila.Control.ChosenText" Color="#FFB597"/>

    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="Aquila.Chrome.Accent"   Color="#FF6B35"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="Aquila.Chrome.Danger"   Color="#E74856"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="Aquila.Chrome.Caution"  Color="#FFB900"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="Aquila.Chrome.Success"  Color="#6CCB5F"/>

    <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         SHAPE — how round the app is, in one place.
         ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

    <CornerRadius x:Key="Aquila.Radius.Card">8</CornerRadius>
    <CornerRadius x:Key="Aquila.Radius.Control">4</CornerRadius>

    <!-- How tall a control is. EVERY control: a button, a combo, a text box, a number box — and it is
         also the width of a square icon button, which is why one number answers both.

         Said rather than left to each control's own padding. The kit sizes each one from its content plus
         whatever padding suits a label, so a combo beside a button came out a pixel or two shorter — near
         enough to look like a mistake and not near enough to look deliberate. A row of controls only lines
         up if something says so. -->
    <sys:Double x:Key="Aquila.Size.Control">32</sys:Double>

    <!-- A radius as a plain number. CornerRadius is what a Border takes and a Rectangle cannot: the ghost
         button draws its dashes with a Rectangle, which wants two doubles. -->
    <sys:Double x:Key="Aquila.Radius.ControlPx">6</sys:Double>

    <!-- The air between stacked cards. From the design canvas, which sets 12 against our 4 — the cards
         were reading as one mass rather than as separate things. -->
    <Thickness x:Key="Aquila.Space.BetweenCards">0,0,0,12</Thickness>

    <Thickness x:Key="Aquila.Space.Tight">6</Thickness>
    <Thickness x:Key="Aquila.Space.Snug">10</Thickness>
    <Thickness x:Key="Aquila.Space.Roomy">14</Thickness>

    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="Aquila.Window.Scrim"     Color="Transparent"/>

    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="Aquila.Scheme.Accent"    Color="#F5A623"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="Aquila.Scheme.Normal"    Color="#FFB765"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="Aquila.Scheme.Elevated"  Color="#F5A623"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="Aquila.Scheme.Alert"     Color="#FF6B35"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="Aquila.Scheme.Critical"  Color="#DA3B01"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="Aquila.Scheme.Series1"   Color="#F5A623"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="Aquila.Scheme.Series2"   Color="#FFD9A8"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="Aquila.Scheme.Series3"   Color="#FFB765"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="Aquila.Scheme.Track"     Color="#21F5A623"/>

</ResourceDictionary>
